# "dia N" names a numbered day of the month: Priberam s.v. "dia", "dia de
# Reis": "Dia 6 de Janeiro, no qual os cristãos celebram a adoração de Jesus
# pelos Reis Magos".  Said without a month ("no dia 3", "o dia 5 do mês"),
# it is the next day with that number, or the anchor's own day when it has
# that number: the month that lacks the day is skipped ("o dia 30 do mês"
# said on 31 January is 30 March).  "próximo" before it ("o próximo dia 5")
# is "Seguinte ao actual" (Priberam s.v. "próximo", 4): the next such day
# after today.
# Portuguese day-of-month label noun -- "dia" ("day") heads the everyday
# idiom "dia N de <mês>" ("day 3 of March" == the 3rd of March), a genuine
# temporal function word (Cunha e Cintra, Nova Gramatica do Portugues
# Contemporaneo, cap. 12, s.v. numerais partitivos e datas; Priberam s.v.
# "dia") -- distinct from a bare ordinal reading, and NOT itself part of the
# date construction's own grammar, so it was left stranded, unconsumed, in a
# range endpoint's remainder even though the date it labels bound correctly
# ("do dia 3 de março ... até ao dia 5 de abril" -- span correct, "dia dia"
# leaked).  Registered as its own marker so range-endpoint resolution
# (:func:`chronologia.extract.timespan._resolve_endpoint`) can fold an
# immediately-adjacent leading "dia" into the consumed set precisely when the
# date it labels actually bound -- never when the date fails to resolve, so a
# "dia" elsewhere in the sentence is untouched.
dia
